      Svix vs Hookdeck: Enterprise Webhook Infrastructure Pricing, Ingestion Latency & Delivery Guarantees
    </h1>

    <!-- Quick Answer Box -->
    <div class="border-l-4 border-orange-500 bg-slate-900/80 border-t border-r border-b border-orange-500/30 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10 shadow-2xl backdrop-blur-sm">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-orange-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">
        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer: Outbound Webhook-as-a-Service vs Inbound Event Gateway
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Choose <strong>Svix</strong> if your product dispatches outbound webhooks to customer endpoints and requires ready-to-use white-label developer portals, open-source Rust backends, and Standard Webhooks compliance. Choose <strong>Hookdeck</strong> if you ingest, queue, transform, and throttle high-volume inbound webhooks from third-party services (Shopify, Stripe) with complex routing and alert rules.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-12">
      <p class="text-lg text-slate-200">
        As distributed software systems evolve from polling-based API architectures to real-time, event-driven topologies, engineering teams encounter a harsh reality: building enterprise-grade webhook infrastructure in-house is deceptively expensive. What begins as an innocent background worker task with a basic retry loop rapidly mutates into a distributed systems nightmare encompassing queue thundering herds, unpredictable consumer downtime, head-of-line blocking, DDoS vulnerabilities, and round-the-clock developer support requests.
      </p>
      <p>
        In response to this operational complexity, two category-defining platforms have emerged: <strong>Svix</strong> and <strong>Hookdeck</strong>. While both provide managed event delivery, their foundational architectures, directional orientations, and pricing models diverge significantly. This technical report provides an exhaustive, benchmarked evaluation of Svix vs Hookdeck across ingestion performance, delivery semantics, failover handling, and enterprise total cost of ownership (TCO).
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Architectural Comparison -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      1. Foundational Architecture: Outbound-First vs Inbound Event Gateway
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The most pivotal architectural distinction between Svix and Hookdeck lies in the primary direction of event transit:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-orange-400 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">Svix: The Outbound Engine</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Svix was engineered from the ground up as a <strong>Webhook-as-a-Service (WaaS)</strong> solution for API companies (e.g., Brex, Supabase, Benchling). When your application needs to notify thousands of external developers that an event occurred (e.g. <code>invoice.paid</code>, <code>build.completed</code>), Svix accepts the dispatch request, signs it cryptographically, fans it out to registered tenant endpoints, executes retries, and renders an embeddable React consumer portal where customers can inspect logs and self-service debug.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800 rounded-xl p-5">
          <div class="text-cyan-400 font-bold text-sm uppercase tracking-wider mb-2 font-mono">Hookdeck: The Inbound Event Gateway</div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Hookdeck is built primarily as an <strong>Event Gateway & Ingestion Proxy</strong>. It stands in front of your internal microservices to ingest webhooks sent by external vendors (Stripe, GitHub, Twilio, Shopify). It absorbs sudden 50,000 req/sec spikes, holds payloads in durable queues, filters and transforms JSON structures via JavaScript rules, and releases them to your internal serverless workers at a predictable, throttled rate.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

