    <!-- H2: Delivery Guarantees & Retry Engines -->
    <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white mb-6 border-b border-slate-800 pb-3">
      2. Delivery Guarantees: Exponential Backoff, Jitter & Circuit Breakers
    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        Both platforms enforce an <strong>at-least-once delivery guarantee</strong>. When an outbound webhook fails due to a network timeout (TCP 504), non-2xx HTTP response (e.g. 500 Internal Server Error, 429 Too Many Requests), or DNS resolution failure, the retry scheduler initiates a resilient backoff sequence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The mathematical difference between basic exponential retry and production backoff is the implementation of <strong>Full Jitter</strong> (as documented by AWS Architecture research):
      </p>
      <div class="bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 font-mono text-xs text-orange-300 my-4">
        <code>SleepInterval = random_between(0, min(MaxBackoff, BaseInterval * 2^attempt))</code>
      </div>
      <p>
        Without randomized jitter, when a major customer endpoint recovers from a 15-minute outage, thousands of retry workers simultaneously strike the customer server at identical harmonic intervals, immediately re-crashing their database. Svix and Hookdeck mitigate this using jittered queues and adaptive concurrency throttling.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- H2: Enterprise Pricing Models & TCO -->
