    </h2>
    <div class="text-slate-300 space-y-4 mb-8">
      <p>
        The decision between Svix and Hookdeck is not a clash of direct feature clones, but an alignment of platform topology:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Adopt Svix If:</strong> You are building a SaaS or API product whose primary requirement is dispatching webhooks to your customers. Svix saves months of frontend and backend development by providing pre-built React components, tenant secret management, and compliance with the Standard Webhooks specification. Additionally, if strict data sovereignty or air-gapped environments mandate on-premise deployment, Svix's open-source AGPL/Apache core is the obvious victor.</li>
        <li><strong>Adopt Hookdeck If:</strong> You are receiving thousands of webhooks from disparate third-party providers (Stripe, Shopify, Zendesk) into internal microservices. Hookdeck excels at shielding your servers from sudden traffic floods, transforming incoming payloads to match internal schemas, and giving your operations team deep observability into third-party vendor reliability.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both platforms definitively outclass the traditional DIY approach of deploying home-grown Kafka/SQS workers, liberating engineering teams to focus entirely on core business differentiation.
      </p>
